    "collected_at": "2026-11-10T17:27:05.163507+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/record-breaking-airbus-a350-flight-lasted-24-hours-crew-witnessed-two-sunsets-and-two-sunrises-278223.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/record-breaking-airbus-a350-flight-lasted-24-hours-crew-witnessed-two-sunsets-and-two-sunrises-278223.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-11-10T15:20:57+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-10T15:20:57+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-10T17:34:08.964915+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-10T17:34:08.964915+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2027 Suzuki SV-7S Unveiled at the EICMA Show in Italy: I’m Suddenly More Nostalgic Than Ever\nRecord-Breaking Airbus A350 Flight Lasted 24+ Hours, Crew Witnessed Two Sunsets and Two Sunrises\n7 photos\nPhoto: Qantas\nThere are long flights, and then there are the flights going from Singapore to Newark, Bangalore to San Francisco, or Dubai to Auckland. These routes, operated by the likes of Singapore Airlines, Air India, or Emirates, usually take around 19 hours, and they put a lot of strain on passengers and crews alike. Imagine, then, how it will be like to fly for close to a full day, without landing.\nThat's what the flag carrier of Australia, Qantas , is planning, as it seeks to directly link, for the first time, Sydney to London and Sydney to New York. That's a distance of around 11,500 miles (18,500 km), which would take some 22 hours to complete.\nAs you're reading this, there is no operational plane capable of doing that. But there soon will be, and now there is an official Guinness World Record for the longest flight by distance by a commercial airliner to prove it.\nThe plane is called Airbus A350-1000ULR (Ultra-Long Range), and it is being specifically developed by the European aerospace giant to serve this particular Qantas requirement. It first flew in the summer of this year, and now it has officially broken the record mentioned above. Ok, maybe not right now, but we're only now learning about it, after its achievement was validated by the International Aeronautical Federation and Flightradar24.\nPhoto: Airbus Wearing callsign AIB35LR, the Airbus -operated flight took off from Melbourne, Australia at 17:27 local time (06:27 UTC) on Monday, 27 July and landed in Toulouse, France at 09:51 am local time (07:51 UTC) on Tuesday, 28 July.\nIt was not a direct, straight-line flight, but one that saw the plane fly over Sydney, head out over the Pacific towards Los Angeles, cross the North American continent, fly over the Atlantic Ocean and then the UK, and finally reach Toulouse. That's a total distance of 14,388 miles (23,155 km) over three continents and two oceans, which were covered in 24 hours and 24 minutes.\nBecause of the particularities of the flight (it flew east across 17 time zones and crossed the international date line), the crew on board, which counted a total of 13 people (three Airbus pilots, two Qantas pilots, seven Airbus engineers, and an International Aeronautical Federation rep), witnessed two sunrises and an equal number of sunsets. And that's something that doesn't happen very often on airplanes.\nPhoto: Airbus The record-setting flight is part of the test campaign for the A350-1000ULR, and the result of a specific request from Qantas to prove the plane can stay in the air this long, in a bid to simulate the very longest commercial operations possible.\nBut how did this plane manage to travel so far without landing or refueling? Well, Airbus did some modifications to the already very capable A350 base aircraft, the most important of which being the fitting of an additional rear center tank capable of carrying enough fuel to guarantee an extra 1,100 miles (1,800 km) of extra flight distance.\nThe plane was supposed to become operational in the Qantas fleet back in 2025, as per the provisions of the Australian airline's Project Sunrise , which is the official name of its quest to get its hands on the longest-range commercial airliner in the world.\nNow, Airbus says it should be ready to fly commercially sometime in 2027, with the first route already decided upon: it will link Sydney to London. In the years to come, 11 more A350-1000ULR airplanes will join the Qantas fleet.\nPhoto: Airbus Normally, an A350-1000 can carry as many as 410 passengers at a time, but the 1000ULR will only transport 238, meaning the lowest seat density of any A350-1000 in service in the world. That is owed in part to the seating design chosen by the Australian airline.\nQantas opted back in 2023 to fit its dozen 1000ULRs with a brand-new, never-before-used Safran-made seating option called Unity. In words that are simple to understand, we're not dealing with seats per se, as you normally get in an aircraft, but miniature studios with their own doors, reclining seats, storage spaces, a hidden wardrobe, and even a 6.6-foot (two meters) bed to go with the 32-inch touchscreen with Bluetooth connectivity.\nEach plane in the fleet will be fitted with 58 of these studios, of which only six will be available in first class, with all the fittings mentioned above. The rest will be deployed in business class in a somewhat scaled-down version, with a seat, storage spaces, a mirror, and a smaller, 18-inch screen. These studios don't come with a bed, but the seats can turn into that when the time comes.\nWhen the 1000ULR goes operational next year, it will become the fourth passenger variant in the A350 family, joining the existing A350-900, A350-900ULR and the A350-1000. A fifth version, this time meant for cargo transport and called A350F, has just begun its flight test program. So far, says Airbus, there are 1,628 orders for the various A350 planes from 70 customers – of that, 740 are already flying in the service of 42 operators.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAirbus Airbus A350 A350-1000ULR\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Sinking of the SS United States Pushed a Day Earlier, Goodbye Ceremony Cancelled",
